    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="18" relname="preparation">Definition:</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="18" relname="span">Intuitiveness is the degree to which the product is deem easy to use and easy to learn for most of its target market or users.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="restatement">In other words, it is a measurement of how simple and comprehensive a product can be for a user.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="21" relname="preparation">Measurement:</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="21" relname="span">The intuitiveness of a product must be defined by the producing company based on business goals.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="elaboration">For example, if the business could have a goal of having 80 percent or more of the tested users grasp the functionality of the product within a 30 minutes of using the it.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="20" relname="span">Thus, the characteristic should be open to the producer to define specifications of desired results.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="7" relname="concession">However, one must remember what Preece (2002) p. 334 states about systems (and or products) ??no system is ever perfect?.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="10" relname="nonvolitional-cause">In his article, Looking for a humane interface: Will computers ever become easy to use?, Jef Raskin, warns of the danger of producers designing products with ease of learning in mind at the expense of productivity.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="25" relname="span">Therefore, producers believe the myth that a product that is easy to use, is probably not easy to learn.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="26" relname="span">In other words, a product with complex functionality can not be easy to learn</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="11" relname="condition">if it is easy to use.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="24" relname="span">Yet, Raskin (1997) underscores that this train of thought is a myth or theory.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="27" relname="span">Intuitiveness is an integral characteristic of usability design.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="14" relname="nonvolitional-result">Thus, designers must make the time to incorporate it into the product equation.</segment>
        <group id="16" type="multinuc"/>
